eason:  \nChecking is optimal. You have position (implied by acting after opponent in prior rounds), a strong draw, and no aggression from opponent. By checking, you control risk and retain optionality. If the river hits your flush or straight, you can then bet or call. If it misses, you can fold cheaply. Raising could work as a bluff but lacks enough fold equity evidence (opponent called earlier). Given your chip lead and game context (late stage, only 3 hands left), preserving chips while maintaining winning chances is key.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
