e turn. Therefore, to call, you need to put in 227. To raise, you must raise by at least double that — so minimum raise is 454 (as listed in valid actions).\n\nYour options: fold (-1), call (0? Wait — clarification needed).\n\nBut according to instruction:  \n"0 for Call/Check" — so even if facing a bet, "0" means call. That seems inconsistent with standard notation, but per your rules:  \n-1 = Fold  \n-2 = All-in  \n0 = Call/Check  \n>0 = Raise\n\nSo in this case, since opponent raised by 227, "0" here must mean **call the 227 bet** (not check). Otherwise, if you could check, the action wouldn\'t require a call.\n\nTherefore:\n- -1: Fold\n- 0: Call 227\n- 454+: Raise (minimum 454, which is 2×227)\n- -2: All-in (you have 19,270, so all-in is ~19k)\n\nYou have strong pot odds if calling: you\'re risking 227 to win what? Let\'s estimate pot size.\n\nPre-flop: blinds assumed (since not stated, but standard). From initial raise of 680, likely big blind is around 200–300. But let\'s reconstruct:\n\nAssume SB = 100, BB = 200 (common in tournaments).  \nOpponent raised to 680 pre-flop → added 480 over BB? Or raised by 680? You said “raised bet by 680” — likely means increased bet by 680, so from BB 200 to 880 total? But you called with action 0 — ambiguous.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the starting pot had 300 (SB+BB), opponent bet 680 total? But says “raised by 680” — so likely added 680 to pot.\n\nBut to simplify: focus on decision.\n\nCurrent situation:\n- You have a flush draw (~9 outs ≈ 19% chance to hit on river, or ~35% if we were calculating from flop, but we\'re on turn — only one card to come).\n- One card to come → probability of hitting a heart on river: 9 outs / 46 unseen cards ≈ 19.6%.\n\nYou need proper pot odds to justify a call.\n\nEstimate pot size before turn betting:\n- Pre-flop: opponent raised by 680, you called → total pre-flop pot: 680*2 = 1,360? Or did they raise to 680? Likely, "raised by 680" means +680 over previous bet.\n\nAssuming blinds: SB 100,